  "last_updated": "2026-05-04",
  "summary": "Items made of precious metals — sterling silver, solid gold (10K-24K), gold-filled, and platinum. The signal is the metal-content marker, not a brand: '.925', 'sterling', '14K gold', '1/20 12K GF', 'platinum'. These items have an intrinsic floor price equal to the melt value of their metal content, often in the $30-300 range for jewelry. Auction listings frequently bury this in title or description ('vintage chain — marked .925'). The matcher catches both explicit metal-content markers AND the abbreviated forms (S925, 14K, GF). CRITICAL: hard negative guards block plated / tone / filled-base impostors — a 'silver-plated bowl' or 'gold-tone necklace' is base-metal commodity, NOT precious metal.",
  "field_workflow": [
    "Photograph the maker's mark / metal-content stamp clearly — usually inside ring band, on clasp, or hidden in a jewelry-piece interior",
    "Sterling: '925' or 'sterling' or '.925' (US standard); '950' (high-grade); '800' (continental European, lower silver content)",
    "Gold karat: 10K (41.7% gold), 14K (58.3%), 18K (75%), 22K (91.7%), 24K (99.9% pure)",
    "Gold-filled: '1/20 12K GF' means 12K gold layer = 1/20 (5%) of total weight; valued at ~5-10% of solid gold equivalent",
    "Gold-plated / RGP / rolled-gold plate: thinner gold layer over base metal, NEAR-ZERO precious-metal value, treat as costume",
    "Platinum: '950', 'PLAT', 'PT950', '950 PT' — denser than silver, ~20× more valuable than silver per gram",
    "Acid test if uncertain — drop of nitric acid + observation chart confirms karat (collector tools $20)",
    "Weight matters: a 30g sterling chain has ~$25 melt floor; a 30g 14K gold chain has ~$1100 melt floor"
  ],
  "skip_list": [
    "Silver-tone (base metal cosmetic finish)",
    "Silver-plated / silverplate / EPNS (electroplated nickel silver)",
    "Gold-tone / gold tone (base metal cosmetic finish)",
    "Gold-plated / gold plate / GP (thin gold layer, near-zero melt value)",
    "Costume jewelry (commodity base metal)",
    "Fashion jewelry (mass-market base metal)",
    "German silver / nickel silver (no actual silver content)",
    "Mexican silver alpaca (alpaca = nickel-zinc-copper alloy, despite the 'Mexican silver' marketing label)"
  ],
